# A bare name in a COLUMN-NAME position (`sort`/`select`/`group`) is taken LITERALLY, and a
# binding of the same name does not win.
#
# ADR 0028 decided the opposite for `where`/`filter`/`with` — a binding in scope wins there,
# because otherwise a library author's parameter names become reserved words in data they
# have never seen. It did not cover these three positions, and this program pins what they
# actually do today, INCLUDING THE SILENT CASE.
#
# THIS GOLDEN IS EXPECTED TO CHANGE when that decision is extended (docs/STATE.md item 2).
# It is here so the change shows up as an explicit, reviewable diff rather than as a silent
# shift in what a program means.

w = "v"
D = dataframe({v: [2, 1, 0], w: [10, 20, 30]})

# THE SILENT CASE. `w` is bound to "v", and the frame also has a column called `w`.
# The column wins, with no error: this sorts by `w`, not by `v`.
print(D.sort(w).column("v"))
# For comparison, both explicit spellings:
print(D.sort(@v).column("v"))
print(D.sort(@w).column("v"))

# Same shape in `select` — the answer is the `w` column, not the `v` the binding named.
print(D.select(w).columns())

# And in `group`, which needs an aggregation to reach: grouping by `w` gives three groups,
# grouping by `g` would give two.
G = dataframe({g: ["a", "a", "b"], w: ["x", "y", "z"], n: [1, 2, 3]})
print(G.group(w).sum(@n).count())
print(G.group(@g).sum(@n).count())

# THE CONTRAST: `with` IS covered by ADR 0028, so there the binding wins over the column.
k = 100
print(D.with({out: k}).column("out"))

# The LOUD case: a binding with NO same-named column errors instead, which is how this is
# usually discovered — and why the silent case above is the worse of the two.
# (`try` binds tighter than `.ok`, so the result has to be bound first.)
k2 = "v"
r = try(D.sort(k2))
print(r.ok)
